{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790344600000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-rereview2.gpt-6-sol.findings.md | sed -n '1,340p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 14:50:56 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 21:50:56 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     3\t\n     4\tConsensus is reached on Rule R’s implementation direction, with one binding choice: **fold Change A into the versioned semantic cutover (Change B)**. Do not ship A as an erratum under the existing contracts. With that choice, no remaining P1 blocks implementation. This is design sign-off, not approval of code that has not yet been written.\n     5\t\n     6\tThe no-shadow proof is sound for execution. Both walker paths and the semantic, stack, and register variable instructions call `engine/resolve-var` ([ast_walker.cljc:379,628](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:379), [semantic.cljc:263](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:263), [stack.cljc:386](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:386), [register.cljc:390](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:390)). If it refuses `yin/def` *before* environment or store lookup, and the valid definition transition never resolves its operator, neither a restored binding nor a store entry can redirect a definition ([engine.cljc:54-73](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:54); [revision 2:28-38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790344000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:28)). Direct store-get is not name resolution. The UCF `unsatisfied-names` helper is a static check with a supplied callback, **not** another `resolve-var` caller ([ucf.cljc:387-395](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc:387)); Fable’s proof should say “every executing VM variable lookup,” not “every conformance check.”\n     7\t\n     8\tChange A cannot be an erratum. UCF explicitly includes resolver precedence in the stamped execution contract and says changing resolution order requires a new revision ([UCF:245-270](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:245)). The old precedence was specified behavior even though it enabled shadowing. Moreover, while `yin/def` remains a primitive, `empty-state` permits a composition-supplied primitive map, so “registry-only” lookup alone does not fix its meaning ([yin.vm.cljc:194-197,1623](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1623)). Fable explicitly offered folding A into B if the strict contract reading prevailed ([revision 2:51-55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790344000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:51)). The coherent split is therefore two commits: atomic Rule R with `v3/v3/b2/r2`, then M2 guard removal.\n     9\t\n    10\tRequired stamps at linker fetch and every direct persistent-code loader are the right rule. Today fetch accepts an omitted contract and the direct semantic and stack loaders do not compare one ([linker.cljc:1184-1193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:1184), [semantic.cljc:713-733](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:713), [stack.cljc:95-114](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:95)). An old-stamped image without definitions must fail by stamp, not by grammar. A caller falsely labelling byte-identical AST content `v3` is asserting a new interpretation; content bytes alone cannot prove its historical provenance. The stack loader’s absent validation is **not the only** current admission gap: the walker’s datom loader reconstructs an AST without whole-tree validation ([ast_walker.cljc:709-721](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:709)). The runtime refusal remains the security backstop; load-time refusal is still an implementation requirement.\n    11\t\n    12\tRemaining non-blocking corrections to carry into the implementation brief:\n    13\t\n    14\tP2 | [revision 2:24-26](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790344000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:24) | The proposed “no `assoc` into `:store` outside `yin.vm.engine`” lint is not a proof of one store-write function: `dao.await` merges into the store, the REPL writes fixed history keys, and the engine itself has resource and scheduler writes outside the proposed helper ([await.cljc:217-223](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/dao/await.cljc:217), [repl.cljc:598-603](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:598), [engine.cljc:404-425](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:404)). | Make the lint an exact allowlist/audit of state-construction writes; route all program writes through `engine/store-put` and assert scheduler update keys cannot be the reserved symbol. This strengthens the separate “store never holds the key” invariant; it is not needed for the no-shadow proof.\n    15\t\n    16\tP2 | [revision 2:46-47](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790344000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:46) | The producer list omits direct `vm/eval`, which creates AST datoms, and the expander’s transformer runner, which constructs and loads a bare row set ([ast_walker.cljc:765-778](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:765), [macro.cljc:723-732](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:723)). | Specify how these trusted fresh-code paths supply the required current contract; never silently assign it to externally supplied persistent datoms or rows. Include both paths in the Change B gate.\n    17\t\n    18\tP2 | [revision 2:55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790344000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55) | “Refused at load” does not describe a raw walker state stepped with directly supplied control; that path has no load event ([ast_walker.cljc:688-691](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:688)). | Test load-time refusal for loader inputs and transition-time refusal for raw control, including reserved store-get/update keys. Keep the old-stamped no-definition image and supplied-primitive cases in the gate.\n    19\t\n    20\tUCF frame encoding, continuation lower-time stamp refusal, and module-store snapshot lowering belong in M4 because UCF lift/lower is not implemented; local parked-during-definition resume belongs in Rule R now ([UCF status:1-5](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1), [revision 2:55-59](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790344000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55)). The deferred UCF tests must remain explicit acceptance obligations, not be treated as passing today.\n    21\t\n    22\tVerdict: READY\n    23\tSign-off: GRANTED\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
