{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc | sed -n '50,145p;570,715p;1110,1150p;1580,1660p'\"","aggregated_output":"    50\t    [vm ast]\n    51\t    \"Convert and evaluate a supplied AST. Does not drain any independently\n    52\t     queued program input; explicit observer coordination does that.\")\n    53\t\n    54\t  (reset\n    55\t    [vm]\n    56\t    \"Reset execution state to a known initial state, preserving loaded program.\")\n    57\t\n    58\t  (halted?\n    59\t    [vm]\n    60\t    \"Returns true if VM has halted (completed or error).\")\n    61\t\n    62\t  (blocked?\n    63\t    [vm]\n    64\t    \"Returns true if VM is blocked waiting for external input.\")\n    65\t\n    66\t  (value\n    67\t    [vm]\n    68\t    \"Returns the current result value, or nil if not yet computed.\"))\n    69\t\n    70\t\n    71\t(defprotocol IVMState\n    72\t  \"CESK state accessor protocol.\n    73\t   Exposes the four components of the CESK machine model.\n    74\t   Representations are VM-specific but the structure is universal.\"\n    75\t\n    76\t  (control\n    77\t    [vm]\n    78\t    \"Returns the current control state (what is being evaluated).\n    79\t     VM-specific: AST node, instruction pointer, node ID, etc.\")\n    80\t\n    81\t  (environment\n    82\t    [vm]\n    83\t    \"Returns the current lexical environment (variable bindings).\")\n    84\t\n    85\t  (store\n    86\t    [vm]\n    87\t    \"Returns the current store (heap/global state).\")\n    88\t\n    89\t  (continuation\n    90\t    [vm]\n    91\t    \"Returns the current continuation (what to do next).\n    92\t     VM-specific: linked frames, stack vector, call-stack, etc.\"))\n    93\t\n    94\t\n    95\t;; =============================================================================\n    96\t;; Rule R: the definition operator is syntax, never a name\n    97\t;; =============================================================================\n    98\t\n    99\t(def definition-operator\n   100\t  \"The one name that is syntax rather than a name: the operator of a\n   101\t   definition `(yin/def <literal-symbol> value)`. It is legal only as the\n   102\t   operator of a two-operand application whose first operand is a literal\n   103\t   symbol other than itself. It is never a binder, a store key, a\n   104\t   definition key, a macro name, or a primitive.\"\n   105\t  'yin/def)\n   106\t\n   107\t\n   108\t(def reserved-names\n   109\t  \"The reserved set: a one-entry contract table. `require` is dynamic and\n   110\t   stays an ordinary primitive.\"\n   111\t  #{definition-operator})\n   112\t\n   113\t\n   114\t(defn reserved-name?\n   115\t  [x]\n   116\t  (contains? reserved-names x))\n   117\t\n   118\t\n   119\t(defn reserved-name-defect\n   120\t  \"The data of a Rule R refusal: `role` names where the reserved name\n   121\t   appeared (`:variable`, `:binder`, `:store-key`, `:definition-key`,\n   122\t   `:definition-shape`, `:primitive`, `:env`, `:store`, `:macro`).\"\n   123\t  [role name]\n   124\t  {:rule :reserved-name, :name name, :role role})\n   125\t\n   126\t\n   127\t(defn refuse-reserved!\n   128\t  \"Throw the Rule R refusal for `name` in `role`, merged with `data`.\"\n   129\t  ([role name] (refuse-reserved! role name {}))\n   130\t  ([role name data]\n   131\t   (throw (ex-info (str \"Reserved name \" name \" used as \"\n   132\t                        (clojure.core/name role))\n   133\t                   (merge data (reserved-name-defect role name))))))\n   134\t\n   135\t\n   136\t(defn check-bindings!\n   137\t  \"Refuse a supplied map (an env, a store, a registry) that binds a\n   138\t   reserved name. Returns `m`.\"\n   139\t  [role m]\n   140\t  (when (map? m)\n   141\t    (when-let [n (some #(when (reserved-name? %) %) (keys m))]\n   142\t      (refuse-reserved! role n)))\n   143\t  m)\n   144\t\n   145\t\n   570\t\n   571\t#?(:clj\n   572\t   (defmacro opcase\n   573\t     \"Dispatch on a numeric opcode using keyword mnemonics.\n   574\t      Resolved at compile-time to a standard 'case' over integers.\"\n   575\t     [op-expr & clauses]\n   576\t     (let [default (when (odd? (count clauses)) (last clauses))\n   577\t           paired (if default (butlast clauses) clauses)\n   578\t           pairs (partition 2 paired)\n   579\t           ;; Keep a local literal map so CLJD macro host compilation\n   580\t           ;; does not depend on resolving `opcode-table` as a host\n   581\t           ;; symbol.\n   582\t           opcodes {:literal 1,\n   583\t                    :load-var 2,\n   584\t                    :move 3,\n   585\t                    :lambda 4,\n   586\t                    :call 5,\n   587\t                    :return 6,\n   588\t                    :branch 7,\n   589\t                    :jump 8,\n   590\t                    :gensym 9,\n   591\t                    :store-get 10,\n   592\t                    :store-put 11,\n   593\t                    :stream-make 12,\n   594\t                    :stream-put 13,\n   595\t                    :stream-cursor 14,\n   596\t                    :stream-next 15,\n   597\t                    :stream-close 16,\n   598\t                    :park 17,\n   599\t                    :resume 18,\n   600\t                    :current-cont 19,\n   601\t                    :tailcall 20,\n   602\t                    :dao.stream.apply/call 21,\n   603\t                    :push 22,\n   604\t                    :halt 23,\n   605\t                    :define 24}\n   606\t           resolved (mapcat (fn [[kw body]] [(get opcodes kw) body]) pairs)]\n   607\t       `(case (int ~op-expr) ~@resolved ~@(when default [default])))))\n   608\t\n   609\t\n   610\t(def schema\n   611\t  \"DaoDB schema for :yin/ AST datoms.\n   612\t   Complete data model for the Universal AST as queryable datoms.\"\n   613\t  {;; Ref attributes (entity references, tempid resolution)\n   614\t   :yin/body {:db/valueType :db.type/ref},\n   615\t   :yin/operator {:db/valueType :db.type/ref},\n   616\t   :yin/operands {:db/valueType :db.type/ref,\n   617\t                  :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/many},\n   618\t   :yin/test {:db/valueType :db.type/ref},\n   619\t   :yin/consequent {:db/valueType :db.type/ref},\n   620\t   :yin/alternate {:db/valueType :db.type/ref},\n   621\t   :yin/source {:db/valueType :db.type/ref},\n   622\t   :yin/target {:db/valueType :db.type/ref},\n   623\t   :yin/val-node {:db/valueType :db.type/ref},\n   624\t   ;; Ground-value attributes. The data model uses Datomic-style schema\n   625\t   ;; definitions. Value types are declared in comments for the data\n   626\t   ;; model.\n   627\t   :yin/type {},      ; keyword (:literal, :variable, :lambda, ...)\n   628\t   :yin/value {},     ; polymorphic (number, string, boolean, keyword, ...)\n   629\t   :yin/name {},      ; symbol\n   630\t   :yin/op {},        ; keyword (dao.stream.apply operation key)\n   631\t   :yin/params {},    ; vector of symbols\n   632\t   :yin/key {},       ; symbol\n   633\t   :yin/prefix {},    ; string\n   634\t   :yin/buffer {},    ; long\n   635\t   :yin/parked-id {}, ; keyword\n   636\t   :yin/tail? {},     ; boolean (tail-position flag)\n   637\t   :yin/root {},      ; boolean — marks the batch's root entity; the last\n   638\t   ;; one in batch order wins (yin.vm.macro.md §2.4)\n   639\t   ;; Macro attributes. Expansion events and their refs belong to the\n   640\t   ;; expander's `event-schema`, not to the Universal AST.\n   641\t   :yin/macro? {},    ; boolean — true if lambda is a macro\n   642\t   :yin/macro-name {} ; symbol — the macro operator's name when it was a\n   643\t   ;; :variable\n   644\t   })\n   645\t\n   646\t\n   647\t(def code-schema\n   648\t  \"DaoDB schema for :yin.code/ linear executable datoms.\n   649\t   A segment entity plus instruction entities ordered by an explicit\n   650\t   :yin.code/pc fact; see docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md §2.\"\n   651\t  {;; Ref attributes (entity references, tempid resolution)\n   652\t   :yin.code/segment {:db/valueType :db.type/ref},      ; instruction → segment\n   653\t   :yin.code/target {:db/valueType :db.type/ref},       ; branch → instruction\n   654\t   :yin.code/body {:db/valueType :db.type/ref},         ; closure → entry\n   655\t   :yin.code/source {:db/valueType :db.type/ref},       ; instruction → AST node\n   656\t   :yin.code/derived-from {:db/valueType :db.type/ref}, ; segment → AST root\n   657\t   ;; Ground-value attributes\n   658\t   :yin.code/type {},      ; keyword (:segment)\n   659\t   :yin.code/length {},    ; long — instruction count; pcs are 0 .. length-1\n   660\t   :yin.code/hash {},      ; string — reserved content address\n   661\t   :yin.code/pc {},        ; long\n   662\t   :yin.code/op {},        ; keyword mnemonic (:const, :call, :halt, ...)\n   663\t   :yin.code/value {},     ; polymorphic literal\n   664\t   :yin.code/name {},      ; symbol\n   665\t   :yin.code/params {},    ; vector of symbols\n   666\t   :yin.code/argc {},      ; long\n   667\t   :yin.code/tail? {},     ; boolean\n   668\t   :yin.code/prefix {},    ; string\n   669\t   :yin.code/key {},       ; symbol\n   670\t   :yin.code/buffer {},    ; long\n   671\t   :yin.code/ffi-op {},    ; keyword (dao.stream.apply operation key)\n   672\t   :yin.code/parked-id {}  ; keyword\n   673\t   })\n   674\t\n   675\t\n   676\t(def ^:private cardinality-many-attrs\n   677\t  \"Attributes with :db.cardinality/many — their values are vectors of refs\n   678\t   that must be expanded into individual :db/add assertions.\"\n   679\t  #{:yin/operands})\n   680\t\n   681\t\n   682\t(defn datoms->tx-data\n   683\t  \"Convert [e a v t m] datoms to DaoDB tx-data [:db/add e a v m].\n   684\t   Expands cardinality-many vector values into individual assertions.\n   685\t\n   686\t   DaoDB implementations like InMemoryDaoDB do not allow nil as a stored\n   687\t   value, so nil-valued assertions are omitted in this index projection.\n   688\t   The canonical datom stream remains unchanged and still carries the\n   689\t   original nil facts.\"\n   690\t  [datoms]\n   691\t  (mapcat (fn [[e a v _t m]]\n   692\t            (cond (and (contains? cardinality-many-attrs a) (vector? v))\n   693\t                  (->> v\n   694\t                       (remove nil?)\n   695\t                       (map (fn [ref] [:db/add e a ref m])))\n   696\t                  (nil? v) []\n   697\t                  :else [[:db/add e a v m]]))\n   698\t          datoms))\n   699\t\n   700\t\n   701\t(defn ast->datoms-with-root\n   702\t  \"Convert AST map into a vector of datoms. A datom is [e a v t m].\n   703\t   Returns [root-id datoms].\n   704\t\n   705\t   The last datom is always `[root-id :yin/root true t m]`, so `index-datoms`\n   706\t   finds the root without depending on emission order.\n   707\t\n   708\t   Entity IDs are tempids (negative integers: -16, -17, -18...) that get resolved\n   709\t   to actual entity IDs when transacted. The transactor assigns real positive IDs.\n   710\t\n   711\t   Options:\n   712\t     :t - transaction ID (default 0)\n   713\t     :m - metadata entity reference (default assert; see dao.datom/reserved)\n   714\t     :id-start - starting entity ID for tempids (default (- datom/first-user-id))\"\n   715\t  ([ast] (ast->datoms-with-root ast {}))\n  1110\t         (let [tag (:type node)\n  1111\t               slots (or (get semantic-bytecode-grammar tag)\n  1112\t                         (throw (ex-info \"Unknown AST node type\"\n  1113\t                                         {:type tag, :node node})))\n  1114\t               body (into [tag] (map #(slot-value tag node %)) slots)\n  1115\t               id (jing/segment-key body)\n  1116\t               row (into [id] body)\n  1117\t               prior (get @rows id)]\n  1118\t           (when (and prior\n  1119\t                      (not (and (= prior row) (same-meta? prior row))))\n  1120\t             (throw (ex-info \"Semantic bytecode address collision\"\n  1121\t                             {:id id, :row row, :prior prior})))\n  1122\t           (swap! rows assoc id row)\n  1123\t           id))]\n  1124\t      (let [root (convert ast)]\n  1125\t        {:root root, :rows @rows}))))\n  1126\t\n  1127\t\n  1128\t(def ^:private source-position-keys\n  1129\t  #{:file :line :column :end-line :end-column})\n  1130\t\n  1131\t\n  1132\t(defn- reader-position\n  1133\t  [node]\n  1134\t  (let [source (:yang/source-position node)\n  1135\t        m (meta node)\n  1136\t        value (merge (select-keys m source-position-keys)\n  1137\t                     (select-keys node source-position-keys)\n  1138\t                     (when (map? source) source))]\n  1139\t    (when (some #(contains? value %) [:file :line :column])\n  1140\t      value)))\n  1141\t\n  1142\t\n  1143\t(defn- frontend-meta-entries\n  1144\t  \"Ordered `[key value]` entries attached to one Universal AST node. Yang\n  1145\t   keys and non-position map metadata are node facts. Reader metadata on a\n  1146\t   name or parameter is qualified by its semantic field so two parameters\n  1147\t   cannot silently claim the same key at one lambda occurrence.\"\n  1148\t  [tag node]\n  1149\t  (let [slots (get semantic-bytecode-grammar tag)\n  1150\t        node-meta (apply dissoc (or (meta node) {}) source-position-keys)\n  1580\t  '[[(p-up ?root ?child ?parent) [(path-pop ?child) ?parent]]\n  1581\t    [(occ-anc ?root ?a ?d) (p-up ?root ?d ?a)]\n  1582\t    [(occ-anc ?root ?a ?d) (p-up ?root ?d ?m) (occ-anc ?root ?a ?m)]\n  1583\t    [(occ-bound? ?root ?path ?name)\n  1584\t     (occ-anc ?root ?lam-path ?path)\n  1585\t     [$occ ?root ?lam-path ?lam]\n  1586\t     [?lam :lambda ?params _]\n  1587\t     [(member? ?params ?name)]]])\n  1588\t\n  1589\t\n  1590\t(def occurrence-fns\n  1591\t  \"The `:fns` map `occurrence-rules` requires (§6.3: quoted-symbol keys):\n  1592\t   membership over a `:syms`/`:nodes` vector, and one step up a §2.5 path\n  1593\t   (nil at the root, where no occurrence tuple carries the answer anyway).\"\n  1594\t  {'member? (fn [coll x] (boolean (some #(= % x) coll))),\n  1595\t   'path-pop (fn [p] (when (pos? (count p)) (subvec p 0 (dec (count p)))))})\n  1596\t\n  1597\t\n  1598\t(defn free-names\n  1599\t  \"§7.7 free-name extraction as a function: every `:variable` name free at\n  1600\t   some occurrence of `root`'s tree. `db` is the row relation\n  1601\t   (`query/relation` over the rows) and `occ` the occurrence relation\n  1602\t   (`occurrences`); both may hold several trees unioned (§6.1) — the rules\n  1603\t   are root-scoped, so only `root`'s binder classifies `root`'s\n  1604\t   occurrences. Conservative by construction: a name whose row is bound at\n  1605\t   one occurrence and free at another is included, never silently dropped\n  1606\t   (S7.6.1). The definition operator is syntax, never a name\n  1607\t   (Rule R), so it is never free.\"\n  1608\t  [db occ root]\n  1609\t  (disj (set (map first\n  1610\t                  (query/collect\n  1611\t                    (query/q '[:find ?name :in $ $occ % ?root\n  1612\t                               :where\n  1613\t                               [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n  1614\t                               [?v :variable ?name]\n  1615\t                               (not (occ-bound? ?root ?path ?name))]\n  1616\t                             db occ occurrence-rules root\n  1617\t                             {:fns occurrence-fns}))))\n  1618\t        definition-operator))\n  1619\t\n  1620\t\n  1621\t;; =============================================================================\n  1622\t;; The footprint table and the §7.7 syntactic extraction queries\n  1623\t;; =============================================================================\n  1624\t;; §7.7: the code part of UCF §7.6.1's conservative fixed point is the\n  1625\t;; queries below, run over the flat rows of every reachable tree ($ast) and\n  1626\t;; the segment-qualified rows of every reachable segment ($code). They\n  1627\t;; supply the fixed point's inputs; the value, name-satisfaction, and\n  1628\t;; module parts remain the walk UCF §7.6.1 specifies. Free names are\n  1629\t;; `free-names` above; everything else the syntactic queries fill is here.\n  1630\t\n  1631\t(def footprint-table\n  1632\t  \"§7.7.1's effect normalization as a data value, keyed by the execution\n  1633\t   contract stamp (UCF §7.3.3) — the effect a syntax tag or mnemonic raises\n  1634\t   is part of that contract, lifted out of the reference machine's hot loop\n  1635\t   (`semantic.cljc`). Two directions: `:tags` over every §2.3 syntax tag\n  1636\t   and `:mnemonics` over every §2.4 mnemonic, each to the set of effect\n  1637\t   identifiers it contributes. Every tag and every mnemonic has a row, so\n  1638\t   \\\"no external effect\\\" is an explicit `#{}`, never an absence; a tag or\n  1639\t   mnemonic outside the table is `:yin.k/undecodable`, the outcome the\n  1640\t   validators give it — and the extraction queries' tag/mnemonic sets are\n  1641\t   table subsets, so over validated rows the lookup is total. The design\n  1642\t   table's tag↔mnemonic correspondence is 1:1 for the effect rows and\n  1643\t   deliberately lumped for the last (5 tags lower to 9 mnemonics; see\n  1644\t   `yin.vm.linearize`), so it is not re-stated as a map here. Its \\\"other\n  1645\t   requirement\\\" column is realized by the extraction queries themselves:\n  1646\t   an FFI op goes to the receiver-capability requirement\n  1647\t   (`:yin.k/ffi-ops` — the pair's own store keys are never a store-slice\n  1648\t   requirement, UCF §7.6.2), a store key to the store slice, a parked id\n  1649\t   to the parked-record obligation, and a call's effects are its callee's\n  1650\t   profile effects (§7.7.2). A `:variable`'s name obligation is\n  1651\t   conditional on freeness (§4.5) — a query (`free-names`), not a\n  1652\t   per-tag footprint fact.\"\n  1653\t  {semantic-contract\n  1654\t   {:tags {:stream/make #{:stream/make},\n  1655\t           :stream/put #{:stream/put},\n  1656\t           :stream/cursor #{:stream/cursor},\n  1657\t           :stream/next #{:stream/next},\n  1658\t           :stream/close #{:stream/close},\n  1659\t           ;; an FFI call is not an effect kind: it goes through the FFI pair\n  1660\t           :dao.stream.apply/call #{},\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
